  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 AM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nVicinity warm front has triggered an increase in mid clouds early\nthis afternoon, which should stick around into the evening. \nTrailing cold front then tracks through the area late tonight, \nshifting winds to the northwest. Front will be moisture starved, \nso not anticipating any precip with its passage. Chance PoPs (30 \nto 50%) are then introduced Wednesday morning as northern stream \ntrough digs down into the Great Lakes. Wind shift will kick off \nlake effect showers during this time, continuing over the typical \nlake effect belts (far north-central Wisconsin and along the Lake \nMichigan shoreline) through Friday due to steady stream of CAA and\nclosed upper low sitting to our east. Cold air intrusion (-7 to \n-9C 850 mb temperatures) will result in the potential for some \nflurries mixing in with light rain at times. However, overall QPF \nshould be fairly limited, with probabilistic guidance showing only\na 20 to 50% signal for receiving 0.1\" of liquid through Friday \nafternoon. If lake effect flurries are able to sustain themselves,\ncould see a tenth or two end up falling, though no accumulations \nare expected.\n\nOutlook... Solid signal (50 to 70%) exists for above normal\ntemperatures through at least the end of the month with El Nino \ndriving the synoptic pattern. Additionally, omega ridge looks to\nset up toward the end of the week, which should result in a\nprolonged period of dry weather to accompany the mild conditions.\nWith D4 drought in far northern Wisconsin, will have to monitor\nfor fire weather concerns over the next several weeks.",
